Hi Chris,

Sorry, me again!

When including interaction terms in rsf.select():
1. If the best fit model selected contains an interaction term, will the corresponding main effects (i.e. contributing predictors) be automatically/always included in that model as well? My internet reading suggests that main effects should typically be included in a model where the corresponding interaction term is included.

2. Given I ultimately want to calculate mean coefficient estimates from all RSF outputs for study population, can coefficients for main effects in best fit models that DO and DO NOT contain interaction terms be considered the same and therefore have their mean calculated? Again, my internet reading indicates that coefficients for main effects in models with interaction terms cannot be interpreted in the same way as main effect coefficients in models without the interaction term, because the unique effect of the predictor variable is no longer limited to that single predictor when an interaction term is included in the model. If this is the case, I may be best served by not including any interaction terms given I want to calculate coefficient mean across all individuals for each parameter/predictor. Otherwise I would have to calculate the mean coefficient for predictors/parameters where an interaction term WAS NOT included in the best fit model, and a separate mean coefficient for the same predictors/parameters where an interaction term WAS included in the best fit model. Is this correct?

3. Just to confirm, if including an offset argument, this means a formula has to be included, and as soon as a formula is included, then the user is responsible for manually standardizing predictors? If a formula is specified but no interaction terms are included will I still need to manually back-transform coefficients/betas?

4. I have now removed GPS fixes/locations that fall within unavailable areas (e.g. water) before creating UDs using akde(), and I have masked unavailable areas in my predictor rasters, so these areas contain NA values. Does this now mean that offset() would be redundant because unavailable areas will already return an NA value and therefore not contribute to predictor assessment/sampling?

Regards,
Luke

